Build a Microsoft Planner Capacity & Workload Dashboard

I want to build a live capacity and workload dashboard using Microsoft Planner as the primary data source.

The purpose is not simply to report how many Planner tasks exist. I want the dashboard to help a manager understand:

* What work the team currently has
* What work is coming
* Who is carrying the workload
* How much estimated effort has been committed
* When the team or an individual is becoming stretched
* Which pieces of work are creating that pressure
* What additional work is sitting in the pipeline
* Whether new work can realistically be moved into Planning

For V1, use Microsoft Planner data only plus any small supporting table required for estimated effort or capacity assumptions.

Do NOT include annual leave, sickness, holidays or other absence calculations in V1. Design the data model so these can be added later in V2.

⸻

1. Current Planner Structure

The Planner currently contains the following buckets:

Wishlist

Work that has been requested or identified but has not yet been committed.

Wishlist work should NOT consume current capacity.

Cyclical

Recurring work that happens monthly, quarterly, annually or on another repeated schedule.

Examples might include:

* Monthly reporting
* Quarterly reporting
* Regular data extracts
* Recurring dashboards
* Routine data quality checks
* Annual processes

Cyclical work SHOULD consume capacity because it represents known committed workload.

Planning

Work that has been scoped, started or committed for delivery.

Planning work SHOULD consume capacity.

Moving something from Wishlist into Planning should effectively mean:

“We are now committing team capacity to this.”

On Hold

Work that has been paused.

On Hold work should normally NOT consume current capacity, but it should remain visible as outstanding demand.

Completed

Finished work.

Completed work should not consume current capacity but should be used for historical delivery metrics.

⸻

2. Core Capacity Concept

The dashboard should calculate workload at:

Person × Week

Weekly capacity should be the main level of analysis.

Monthly, 4-week, 8-week and 12-week views can then be calculated from weekly capacity.

Do not rely primarily on task count.

The dashboard needs to consider the estimated amount of work associated with each Planner task.

For example:

Three tasks estimated at:

* 0.5 day
* 1 day
* 1 day

should not be treated as equivalent to three tasks estimated at:

* 5 days
* 8 days
* 10 days.

