20. Dashboard UX

The dashboard should look modern, simple and executive-friendly.

Avoid excessive charts.

Prioritise:

1. Capacity status
2. Where capacity pressure occurs
3. Who is affected
4. What work causes it
5. What future demand exists

Use strong visual hierarchy.

Use status indicators consistently:

Green = Capacity available
Amber = Filling up
Orange = Stretched
Red = Over capacity

Users should be able to understand the overall team position within approximately 10 seconds of opening the dashboard.

⸻

21. Live / Refresh Architecture

The solution should behave as close to live as reasonably possible.

Microsoft Planner should remain the system where users manage their work.

The dashboard should automatically ingest changes to:

* Task
* Bucket
* Owner
* Start Date
* Due Date
* Completion
* Priority
* Estimated effort

Where true event-based updates are not available, use a scheduled refresh/synchronisation.

Design this so refresh frequency can later be adjusted without rebuilding the solution.

